\details{
A tibble has a single header row, so the two-row banner header (each grouping
variable's name spanning its items) cannot be stored in the data itself: the
wide return carries the grouping in the \code{"banner_spanners"} attribute and
\code{flextable = TRUE} renders the real spanning header. Column percentages are
computed \emph{within} each banner column, so every column (Overall included) sums
to about 100 down each question block. Registered orders (\code{\link[=register_order]{register_order()}})
set the item and banner-column ordering automatically. Passing the same
selection to \code{rows} and \code{cols} gives the full every-question-by-every-question
matrix (a variable is never crossed with itself). Supplying only the data
frame does this automatically: every variable with at most \code{max_levels}
distinct answers becomes both a stub and a banner group, numeric scales are
added as stub statistics blocks, and identifier / free-text columns are
skipped (and named in a message). Check-all-that-apply blocks (columns sharing
a prefix that each hold one option or blank, such as \verb{motivations_*}) are
recognised as a single multi-select stub question and tabulated with
\code{\link[=calc_percentage_multi]{calc_percentage_multi()}}, whose base is the respondents who picked any option
(so those rows can sum past 100). Multi-select blocks appear as stubs only, not
banner groups, and are unweighted.

A full every-variable banner is one \code{\link[=crosstab]{crosstab()}} per question per grouping
variable, so a wide survey takes minutes rather than seconds. In an
interactive session it reports which question it is on, with an estimate of
the time left; \code{ezrsurvey_options(progress = FALSE)} turns that off, and
scripts are silent by default. \code{checkpoint = TRUE} additionally saves each
question as it completes, so re-running the identical call after an
interruption resumes instead of starting again. The managed file is named
after the run's own fingerprint, so a call finds its own interrupted run and
a call with different data or arguments never sees it; pass a path instead if
you would rather choose the location. Either way the file is yours to delete
once the table is built.
